# AWS Services Planning

## 1. Purpose

AWS services are considered as the target cloud infrastructure for the intelligent auto-scaling system. The current implementation uses a mock Auto Scaling Group to test the RL decision-making process before connecting the trained agent to actual AWS resources.

## 2. Planned AWS Services

### Amazon EC2

Amazon EC2 will provide the compute instances that run the application workload.

### Elastic Load Balancing

Elastic Load Balancing can distribute incoming requests across available EC2 instances and support workload distribution when the number of instances changes.

### EC2 Auto Scaling

AWS Auto Scaling will be used as the target infrastructure for applying the scaling decisions generated by the RL agent.

The project uses an Auto Scaling Group with a configured minimum and maximum instance capacity.

### Amazon CloudWatch

CloudWatch can provide monitoring information such as CPU utilization and other system metrics. These metrics can be used as workload information for future RL-based scaling decisions.

### Amazon RDS

Amazon RDS can provide managed database infrastructure for applications that require persistent database storage.

### Amazon S3

Amazon S3 can be used for storing datasets, trained models, experiment results, logs, or other project artifacts.

## 3. Current Implementation

The current implementation focuses on the reinforcement learning decision-making pipeline. A mock AWS Auto Scaling Group is used to simulate scale-in, maintain, and scale-out operations.

The current flow is:

Workload Data → FastAPI → Q-learning Agent → Scaling Decision → Mock Auto Scaling Group

## 4. Future Integration

The next stage of the project can replace the mock scaling component with AWS Auto Scaling API calls. This would allow the trained RL agent to interact with an actual AWS Auto Scaling Group.

## 5. AWS Region

The project AWS resources are configured in the Asia Pacific (Mumbai) region (`ap-south-1`).
